---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const entry = {
  "id": "harvard_vi4_440",
  "series": "Band VI 4 (1677 - 1690)",
  "seq": 440,
  "text": "[[1976.1]] <b>362.  Principia mechanica ex metaphysicis dependere [Sommer 1678 bis Winter 1680/81 (?)]</b>\n [[1976.2]] <tab/>[Sommer 1678 bis Winter 1680/81 (?)] </p>\n<p> [[1976.8]] Tempus erat quo credebam, omnia Motuum Phaenomena principiis pure Geometricis\n[[1976.9]] explicari posse, nullis Metaphysicis propositionibus assumtis, et concursuum\n[[1976.10]] Leges ex solis motuum compositionibus pendere; sed hoc impossibile esse profundiori\n[[1976.11]] meditatione deprehendi; didicique veritatem tota mechanica potiorem, scilicet omnia\n[[1976.12]] quidem in natura explicari posse Mechanice, sed ipsa principia mechanica ex metaphysicis\n[[1976.13]] et quodammodo moralibus, id est contemplatione causae efficientis et finalis, Dei\n[[1976.14]] scilicet perfectissime operantis, dependere; ex caecis autem compositionibus motuum\n[[1976.15]] nullo modo deduci posse. Et proinde impossibile esse, ut nihil sit in mundo praeter\n[[1976.16]] materiam ejusque variationes quod Epicuri secta statuebat. Quod ut clarius appareat\n[[1976.17]] repetam prius paucis, quae olim statui posse credideram, et deinde dicam quid me ab ea\n[[1976.18]] sententia abduxerit. </p>\n<p> [[1976.19]] Ponamus <b>duo corpora</b> <i>A</i> et <i>B</i> in\n[[1976.20]] fig. 1. quae instar punctorum hoc loco\n[[1976.21]] considero <b>concurrere sibi directe\n[[1976.22]] in linea recta</i> <i>AB</i>, et eodem\n[[1976.23]] momento egressa ex locis <i>₁A</i> et <i>₁B</i>, ac\n[[1977.1]] <b>motu aequabili</b> pergentia concurrere in locis <i>₂A</i> et <i>₂B</i>, et proinde celeritates eorum\n[[1977.2]] repraesentari per rectam <i>₁A₂A</i>, et <i>₁B₂B</i> quas eodem tempore absolverunt. </p>\n<p> [[1977.3]] <b>Dicebam tardius <i>B</b> <b>abripi a celeriore <i>A</b>, et a loco concursus <i>₂A₂B</i> simul\n[[1977.4]] ferri in <i>₃A₃B</i>, celeritate <i>₂A₃A</i> (vel <i>₂B₃B</i>) quae sit <b>differentia priorum celeritatum</b>\n[[1977.5]] <i>₁A₂A</i>, et <i>₁B₂B</i> seu tanto tempore quo ante concursum venerant simul <i>A</i> ex <i>₁A</i> in <i>₂A</i>, et <i>B</i> ex\n[[1977.6]] <i>₁B</i> in <i>₂B</i>, nunc post concursum simul ventura esse, <i>A</i> ex <i>₂A</i> in <i>₃A</i>, et <i>B</i> ex <i>₂B</i> in <i>₃B</i>, et <i>₂A₃A</i>\n[[1977.7]] vel <i>₂B₃B</i>, fore aequalem <i>₁A₂A</i> minus <i>₁B₂B</i>. </p>\n<p> [[1977.8]] Demonstrationem ex eo petebam, quod supponebam in corpore nihil aliud considerari\n[[1977.9]] posse, quam molem, hoc est extensionem et impenetrabilitatem, sive quod eodem\n[[1977.10]] redit spatii seu loci impletionem; in motu autem nihil aliud considerari posse quam\n[[1977.11]] mutationem eorum quae diximus, seu mutationem loci. Quod si ergo nihil aliud affirmare\n[[1977.12]] volumus, quam quod ex his notionibus sequitur, dicemus causam cur corpus unum aliud\n[[1977.13]] impellat, petendam esse, ex natura impenetrabilitatis, dum enim corpus <i>A</i> impingit in\n[[1977.14]] corpus <i>B</i>, et ipsum penetrare non potest, non potest motum suum continuare, quin ipsum\n[[1977.15]] secum abripiat. Cumque in ipso momento concursus pergere conetur, conabitur alterum\n[[1977.16]] secum abripere; id est incipiet abripere, seu\n[[1977.17]] alteri quoque conatum imprimet eadem celeritate\n[[1977.18]] et directione eundi, omnis enim conatus\n[[1977.19]] est initium actionis, ergo et initium continet\n[[1977.20]] effectus, seu passionis in eo in quod agitur. Et\n[[1977.21]] is conatus successum habebit plenum, atque <i>A</i> quidem eadem celeritate perget, <i>B</i> autem\n[[1977.22]] post concursum eadem celeritate et directione movebitur, qua <i>A</i> venerat; si modo nihil\n[[1977.23]] obstet; nihil autem obstat, si corpus <i>B</i> ante concursum ponatur esse in quiete ut in fig. 2.\n[[1977.24]] (ubi puncta <i>₁B</i> et <i>₂B</i> coincidunt), sive indifferens ad quemlibet motum recipiendum,\n[[1977.25]] itaque in casu figurae 2. ipsi <i>₁A₂A</i> aequabitur recta <i>₂A₃A</i> vel <i>₂B₃B</i>. Nam dicere materiam\n[[1978.1]] motui resistere et totum ex <i>A</i> et <i>B</i> compositum nunc tardius moveri, quam antea solum <i>A</i>,\n[[1978.2]] est aliquid asserere, quod ex simplici natura corporis et motus, qualem supra assumsimus,\n[[1978.3]] si in ea nihil aliud quam spatii impletionem et mutationem intelligimus, duci non potest. </p>\n<p> [[1978.4]] Si vero in fig. 3. ponamus duo corpora sibi occurrere celeritate aequali, post concursum\n[[1978.5]] ambo quiescent. Nam corpus <i>A</i> in momento concursus duos habebit conatus,\n[[1978.6]] unum pergendi celeritate qua venit, nempe\n[[1978.7]] celeritate <i>₁A₂A</i>, alterum regrediendi celeritate\n[[1978.8]] qua alterum <i>B</i> venit, <i>₁B₂B</i>, quae celeritati\n[[1978.9]] <i>₁A₂A</i> est aequalis, itaque moveri intelligetur\n[[1978.10]] duobus motibus oppositis aequalibus,\n[[1978.11]] ut uterque conatus effectum habere intelligatur id est quiescet. Nam si in fig. 4. in\n[[1978.12]] navi <i>LM</i> pila <i>C</i> a prora ad puppim currat celeritate <i>₁C₂C</i>, et interim navis progrediatur\n[[1978.13]] celeritate <i>₁M₂M</i>, quae sit ipsi <i>₁C₂C</i> aequalis,\n[[1978.14]] tunc pila omni suo conatu locum non mutabit,\n[[1978.15]] sed eidem puncto <i>N</i> ripae immotae\n[[1978.16]] respondebit. Et quod de corpore <i>A</i> diximus\n[[1978.17]] in fig. 3 dicendum erit et de altero <i>B</i>. </p>\n<p> [[1978.18]] Si vero ut in fig. 1. alterutrum corpus\n[[1978.19]] celerius moveatur id vincet, et ambo pergent\n[[1978.20]] directione quidem celerioris, differentia\n[[1978.21]] autem celeritatum. Nam corpus quidem <i>A</i>\n[[1978.22]] momento concursus duos habebit conatus, unum pergendi celeritate ut <i>₁A₂A</i>, majore;\n[[1978.23]] regrediendi celeritate ut <i>₁B₂B</i>, minore; corpus vero <i>B</i> itidem duos sed contra pergendi\n[[1978.24]] celeritate minore <i>₁B₂B</i>, regrediendi celeritate majore <i>₁A₂A</i>. Qui conatus ut omnes suum\n[[1978.25]] effectum sortiantur, rursus motus istos inter se componamus, quos conatus isti exigunt.\n[[1978.26]] Semper autem si corpus moveatur duobus motibus oppositis simul, in summa movebitur\n[[1978.27]] directione majoris, at differentia celeritatum, id est ambo simul ibunt in fig. 1. ex <i>₂A₂B</i> in\n[[1978.28]] <i>₃A₃B</i> celeritate <i>₂A₃A</i> vel <i>₂B₃B</i>, quae est <i>₁A₂B</i> minus <i>₁B₂B</i>. Quod rursus exemplo navis\n[[1978.29]] intelligemus in fig. 5, nam si ripa sit <i>NPQR</i>, navis autem <i>LM</i> in flumine pergat ex <i>₁L₁M</i> in\n[[1979.1]] <i>₂L₂M</i> celeritate <i>NP</i>, secumque ferat corpus <i>C</i>\n[[1979.2]] in navi interim procurrens a prora versus\n[[1979.3]] puppim celeritate <i>₁C₂C</i>, manifestum est id\n[[1979.4]] duabus ferri celeritatibus oppositis, una\n[[1979.5]] navis <i>PR</i>, majore (aequali ipsi <i>NP</i>) altera\n[[1979.6]] minore propria contraria <i>QP</i> (aequali\n[[1979.7]] ipsi <i>₁C₂C</i>) ergo revera transferri respectu\n[[1979.8]] ripae ex loco <i>Q</i> in locum <i>R</i>, in easdem partes\n[[1979.9]] in quas tendit navis, seu directione\n[[1979.10]] celeritatis majoris, celeritate <i>QR</i> quae est differentia celeritatum <i>PR</i> et <i>QP</i>. </p>\n<p> [[1979.11]] Superest unus adhuc casus, si in fig. 6. corpora <i>A</i> et <i>B</i> tendant ad easdem partes in\n[[1979.12]] eadem recta, sed <i>A</i> celerius assequatur ipsum <i>B</i> tardius inque ipsum ita impingat tunc\n[[1979.13]] ambo pergent simul celeritate majoris, seu celeritate <i>₂A₃A</i> vel <i>₂B₃B</i> quae sit aequalis\n[[1979.14]] celeritati <i>₁A₂A</i>. Seu ut in praecedentibus differentia celeritatum detrahenda est celeritati\n[[1979.15]] majori cum sibi occurrerent, ita nunc eadem adjicienda\n[[1979.16]] est celeritati minori, cum celerius incurrit\n[[1979.17]] in tardius. Nam impingit in ipsum solo excessu\n[[1979.18]] celeritatis, quatenus enim eandem celeritatem\n[[1979.19]] habent unum in alterum non agit, perinde ac si in eadem navi lata essent motu ejus\n[[1979.20]] communi, in ipsa autem navi tardius quidem quiescere, celerius autem differentia celeritatum\n[[1979.21]] in ipsum incurrere poneretur. Si vero simpliciter posuissemus celerius totam suam\n[[1979.22]] celeritatem imprimere tardiori antecedenti, tunc non simul pergerent, quod tamen hic\n[[1979.23]] semper alias fit, sed celerius suam retineret velocitatem, tardius summa velocitatum\n[[1979.24]] moveretur, et ita fieret celerius. </p>\n<p> [[1980.1]] Haec quae de concursu corporum ita conclusimus, in eo maxime ab experientia\n[[1980.2]] dissentiunt, quod magnitudine corporis movendi aucta, ut in fig. 2. non ideo statuitur\n[[1980.3]] celeritatis minui; nam de reliquo, eadem fere fiunt, si duo corpora mollia et elastro\n[[1980.4]] carentia, cujus vi post concursum dissilire cogantur, concurrere sinamus, ut duos globos\n[[1980.5]] argillaceos chartae involutos, ex filis suspensos; nisi quod divisio per eorum summam\n[[1980.6]] instituenda est, seu celeritas dimidianda est, cum corpora sunt aequalia. Cogitabam autem\n[[1980.7]] etsi in statu corporum extra systema posito, et ut ita dicam rudi contingerent, quae\n[[1980.8]] concluseram, ita, ut corpus maximum quiescens a minimo impingente eadem qua minimum\n[[1980.9]] celeritate abriperetur, in systemate tamen seu in corporibus circa nos tale quid fore\n[[1980.10]] perabsurdum, ita enim minimo negotio maxima turbarentur, et proinde variis artificiis\n[[1980.11]] hunc effectum impediri. Corpora enim Elastro esse praedita, et flexilia, et partem impelli\n[[1980.12]] saepe toto non impulso; sed cum cogitarem quomodo in universum ratio reddi posset\n[[1980.13]] ejus, quod in summa experimur, ut aucta mole minuatur celeritas, uti videmus navem\n[[1980.14]] eandem secundo flumine eo ferri tardius, quo magis oneratur, haesi utique, atque omnibus\n[[1980.15]] frustra tentatis deprehendi hanc ut ita dicam inertiam corporum, ex sola illa initio\n[[1980.16]] posita materiae et motus notione, qua materia quidem intelligitur id quod extensum est\n[[1980.17]] seu spatium replet; motus autem spatii seu loci mutatio; deduci non posse, sed praeter\n[[1980.18]] haec quae ex sola extensione ejusque variatione seu modificatione deducuntur, adjiciendas\n[[1980.19]] atque agnoscendas esse in corporibus notiones sive formas quasdam ut ita dicam\n[[1980.20]] immateriales sive ab extensione independentes; quas appellare possis potentias, quibus\n[[1980.21]] celeritas magnitudini attemperatur, quae potentiae [n]on in motu, imo nec in [co]natu seu\n[[1980.22]] motus [initi]o, sed in causa sive ratione intrinseca [motus] ea qua opus est lege continuandi\n[[1980.23]] consistunt. Et in eo erratum est, quod motum quidem consideravere autores, sed\n[[1980.24]] non potentiam motricem seu motus rationem quam etsi a Deo rerum autore et gubernatore\n[[1980.25]] petamus non tamen in ipso Deo esse, sed ab eo in rebus produci conservarique\n[[1980.26]] intelligendum est. Unde etiam non eandem quantitatem motus (quod plerosque decepit),\n[[1980.27]] sed potentiae, in mundo servari, ostendemus. </p>\n",
  "number": "362.",
  "title": "Principia mechanica ex metaphysicis dependere",
  "date": "[Sommer 1678 bis Winter 1680/81 (?)]"
};
---

<Layout title={`${entry.title || entry.id} - Leibniz Writings`}>
  <article data-pagefind-body>
    <header class="entry-header">
      <h1 data-pagefind-meta="title">{entry.title || entry.id}</h1>

      <div class="entry-meta">
        {entry.date && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Date:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="date">{entry.date}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Series:</strong>
          <span data-pagefind-meta="series">{entry.series}</span>
        </div>

        {entry.number && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Number:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="number">{entry.number}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Sequence:</strong>
          <span>{entry.seq}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- View Toggle -->
    <div class="view-controls">
      <button id="translation-view-btn" class="view-btn active">Translation View</button>
      <button id="scholarly-view-btn" class="view-btn">Scholarly View</button>
    </div>

    <div class="entry-content" data-pagefind-meta="excerpt:362. Principia mechanica ex metaphysicis dependere [Sommer 1678 bis Winter 1680/81 (?)] [Sommer 1678 bis Winter 1680/81 (?)] Tempus erat quo credebam, omnia Motuum Phaenomena principiis pure Geometricis explicari posse, nullis Metaphysicis propositionibus assumtis, et concursuum Leges ex solis motuu...">

      <!-- Translation-friendly continuous text -->
      <div id="translation-view" class="text-view active">
        <div class="translation-notice">
          <p><strong>📖 Translation View:</strong> Continuous text optimized for browser translation tools.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="continuous-text" translate="yes" lang="unknown" id="continuous-text">
          <!-- Will be populated by JavaScript -->
        </div>
        <div class="line-numbers-reference" id="line-numbers-ref">
          <!-- Will be populated by JavaScript -->
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Detailed scholarly view with line numbers -->
      <div id="scholarly-view" class="text-view">
        <div class="scholarly-notice">
          <p><strong>📚 Scholarly View:</strong> Original text with line number annotations preserved.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="annotated-text" id="annotated-text">
          {entry.text.split('\n').map(paragraph =>
            paragraph.trim() && <p set:html={paragraph} />
          )}
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </article>

  <nav class="entry-navigation">
    <a href="/" class="back-link">← Back to Search</a>
  </nav>
</Layout>

<style>
  .entry-header {
    margin-bottom: 30px;
    padding-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border);
  }

  .entry-header h1 {
    color: var(--primary);
    font-size: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .entry-meta {
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .meta-item {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .meta-item strong {
    color: var(--text);
    min-width: 80px;
  }

  .meta-item span {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    flex: 1;
  }

  .entry-content {
    margin-bottom: 40px;
  }

  .text-content {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 30px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .text-content :global(p) {
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }

  .text-content :global(p:last-child) {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .text-content :global(b) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .entry-navigation {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-top: 20px;
  }

  .back-link {
    color: var(--primary);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 500;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    transition: color 0.2s;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--primary-dark);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  /* View Toggle Controls */
  .view-controls {
    display: flex;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-bottom: 20px;
  }

  .view-btn {
    background: transparent;
    border: 2px solid var(--border);
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    transition: all 0.2s;
  }

  .view-btn:hover {
    border-color: var(--primary);
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .view-btn.active {
    background: var(--primary);
    border-color: var(--primary);
    color: white;
  }

  /* Text Views */
  .text-view {
    display: none;
  }

  .text-view.active {
    display: block;
  }

  /* Translation View */
  .translation-notice, .scholarly-notice {
    background: var(--background);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    font-size: 14px;
  }

  .continuous-text {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 2px solid var(--primary);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 25px;
    line-height: 1.8;
    font-size: 16px;
    color: var(--text);
    margin-bottom: 15px;
  }

  .line-numbers-reference {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 13px;
    padding: 10px;
    background: var(--background);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  /* Scholarly View */
  .annotated-text {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 20px;
  }

  .text-line {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  .line-refs {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 4px;
    min-width: 80px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .line-num {
    background: var(--primary);
    color: white;
    padding: 2px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-family: monospace;
  }

  .line-content {
    flex: 1;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .line-content :global(b) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .line-content :global(i) {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .entry-meta {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .view-controls {
      flex-direction: column;
    }

    .text-line {
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 6px;
    }

    .line-refs {
      min-width: auto;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Process text for translation view
  function processTextForTranslation(text) {
    if (!text) return { translationText: '', lineNumbers: [] };

    const lines = text.split('\n');
    let translationText = '';
    const lineNumbers = [];

    lines.forEach(line => {
      const trimmedLine = line.trim();
      if (!trimmedLine) return;

      // Extract line numbers
      const lineNumberMatch = trimmedLine.match(/\[\[(\d+\.\d+)\]\]/g);
      const extractedNumbers = lineNumberMatch ?
        lineNumberMatch.map(m => m.replace(/\[|\]/g, '')) : [];

      // Remove line number markers and HTML tags for continuous text
      let contentOnly = trimmedLine
        .replace(/\[\[\d+\.\d+\]\]/g, '')
        .replace(/<\/?[^>]+(>|$)/g, '')
        .replace(/\s+/g, ' ')
        .trim();

      if (contentOnly) {
        if (translationText) translationText += ' ';
        translationText += contentOnly;
        lineNumbers.push(...extractedNumbers);
      }
    });

    return {
      translationText: translationText.trim(),
      lineNumbers: lineNumbers
    };
  }

  // View switching and text processing
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    const translationBtn = document.getElementById('translation-view-btn');
    const scholarlyBtn = document.getElementById('scholarly-view-btn');
    const translationView = document.getElementById('translation-view');
    const scholarlyView = document.getElementById('scholarly-view');
    const continuousTextDiv = document.getElementById('continuous-text');
    const lineNumbersRef = document.getElementById('line-numbers-ref');

    // Process the text for translation view
    if (continuousTextDiv && lineNumbersRef && entry && entry.text) {
      const processed = processTextForTranslation(entry.text);
      continuousTextDiv.textContent = processed.translationText;

      if (processed.lineNumbers.length > 0) {
        lineNumbersRef.innerHTML = `<small><strong>Line numbers:</strong> ${processed.lineNumbers.join(', ')}</small>`;
      }
    }

    // View switching functionality
    if (translationBtn && scholarlyBtn && translationView && scholarlyView) {
      translationBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        translationBtn.classList.add('active');
        scholarlyBtn.classList.remove('active');
        translationView.classList.add('active');
        scholarlyView.classList.remove('active');
      });

      scholarlyBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        scholarlyBtn.classList.add('active');
        translationBtn.classList.remove('active');
        scholarlyView.classList.add('active');
        translationView.classList.remove('active');
      });
    }
  });
</script>